this is a review of the dangerous allure  of advanced artificial intelligence life  3.0 being human in the age of artificial  intelligence by Max tegmark review by  Bryan Keating physics today 2018 Albert  Einstein famously quipped I know not  with what weapons World War 3 will be  fought but world war 4 will be fought  with sticks and stones in life 3.0 max  tegmark MIT professor makes a persuasive  case that artificial intelligence AI may  be a weapon or the enemy that reduces us  to those sticks and stones unless we  humans and our AI partners act with  extreme care tegmark also the author of  our mathematical universe my quest for  the ultimate nature of reality clearly  has found a formula for a successful  book a provocative subject a well  reasoned argument and easy-to-understand  classification scheme for example a four  level multiverse in my mathematical  universe three forms of life and life  3.0 whimsical illustrations and personal  anecdotes that humanize science tech  mark also throws in something that's  become close to a personal trademark a  vivid fictional opening scene that reads  as if it had been ripped from a  screenplay tegmark defines life rather  narrowly as activities that can process  information and can retain said  informations complexity after  replication by that definition it may  not be obvious how a bacterium which  most people would say is alive actually  qualifies as life in contrast an  electronic thermostat clearly processes  complex information and so it should  qualify under his definition tegmark  builds on the idea of life as  information processing to construct a  three form schema life 1.0 is the most  basic biological stage life 2.0 cannot  design its own hardware but can alter  its software for example by learning a  new language tegmark then leads us to a  life beyond human superhuman AI or life  3.0 tegmark conceives of life 3.0 as  entities that not only can reinvent  themselves via software enhancements but  can also upgrade their performance  hardware although we humans can install  artificial prosthesis we cannot as yet  duplicates or create improved versions  of ourselves at will as such we only  qualify at best as life 2.1 after  dispensing with definition tegmark takes  us through the history of computing an  AI the book life 3.0 is less about the  design or implementation of third  generation of life though such topics  are thoroughly discussed then it is  about life 3.0 implications much has  been made about the perils of runaway AI  Elon Musk for example has called it  potentially more dangerous than nukes  and Stephen Hawking said it could spell  the end of the human race tegmark is  more optimistic than Hawking or musk  about the future of humans living with  or under life 3.0 still his is a  cautionary tale and he warns about the  potential dangers should AI develop  goals that are not aligned with  humanity's consider for example what  would happen if terrorists were to equip  autonomous drones with weapons creating  weaponized AI life 3.0 urges us to  prepare for an onslaught of super  intelligent AI now rather than waiting  and with luck learning from our mistakes  as we humans typically do his argument  is clear we can afford neither  procrastination normos takes this time  tegmark presents a thorough account of  massive flaws in several previous human  AI interactions that have led to  disaster in part because of code errors  such as the tragic 1979 death of a Ford  Motor Company workers struck in the head  by a robotic arm but he suggests that  future AI will be super resilient immune  to software bugs and what about  competition what happens when two AI  square off against each other in a  battle for scarce resources the book is  not without a few minor irritations at  first glance the decimal place and his  classification scheme seemed superfluous  surely tegmark does not mean that  there's an infinitude of different  levels of life but in fact he does seem  to assert such a continuum with life 2.1  being humans who are able to learn  languages and skills and thus improve  their software and also make upgrades to  their hardware with prostheses life 3.0  will be able to replicate itself that is  the information it contains we all know  that life can sometimes be irrational  but is there really any difference  between life 3.1 and life 3.141592  there is also a bit too much of hey  geography of Elon Musk too many plugs  for the funding of the future of life  Institute of which tegmark is a founder  and too many photos and lists of people  discussing AI at workshops after dinner  most of these could easily been  jettisoned  to reduce the books have to accentuate  material more essential to tag marks  arguments alas tegmark offers no  solutions for AI life-forms that plagued  us today such as AI chat BOTS or  irritating Microsoft paperclips but he  does offer hope if we act quickly and  intelligently this can be accomplished  will there be a life 4.0 or even a life  3.1 it is hard to know perhaps if  humanity takes tag marks call-to-action  seriously there may be more than just  hope there may actually be a chance for  a future
 

